<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · island</p><h1 class="enc-title">Lau Islands</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Lau group</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Lau Islands are a scattered group of about sixty islands in eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fiji">Fiji</a>, lying towards Tonga, with Lakeba and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vanua-balavu">Vanua Balavu</a> among the largest. Close contact with Tonga brought Tongan settlers and Wesleyan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a>, and in the mid-19th century the Tongan noble <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/enele-maafu">Enele Maʻafu</a> became the dominant power there. They form Fiji's Lau Province, whose chiefs, notably <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ratu-sir-kamisese-mara">Ratu Sir Kamisese Mara</a>, have been prominent in modern Fijian politics.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lau-islands.jpg" alt="Lau Islands" width="320" height="371" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: CIA World Factbook map · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:LauIslandsMap.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
